.TH "BULKRENAME" 1 "2026-09-30" "toolbelt 0.1.0" "toolbelt manual"
.SH NAME
bulkrename \- rename many files with a pattern or in your editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bulkrename [options] 's/from/to/' file ...
bulkrename [options] -e | --lower | --upper | --spaces | --number file ...
bulkrename --undo
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBbulkrename\fR gives many files new names in one go. You describe the new names with a sed expression, with your editor, or with a few ready transforms such as \fB\-\-lower\fR. It then shows every old and new name, asks once, and renames them all.
.PP
It checks every rename before it moves anything. Two files that would get the same name, a new name that already belongs to another file, and a name with a \fB/\fR in it all stop the run with exit 4, and nothing is renamed. A rename never overwrites a file.
.PP
Only the file name changes. A file in \fBphotos/2026/\fR stays in that folder, and the expression never sees the folder part, so \fBs/^IMG_/trip\-/\fR works on \fBphotos/2026/IMG_4410.jpg\fR the same as on \fBIMG_4410.jpg\fR.
.PP
After each run, \fBbulkrename\fR saves the list of renames. \fBbulkrename \-\-undo\fR puts the old names back, from any folder.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS How the new names are made
.TP
\fB's/from/to/'\fR
A sed expression. It runs once per name, with extended regular expressions, as in \fBsed \-E\fR.
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-edit\fR
Open the names in \fB$VISUAL\fR or \fB$EDITOR\fR, or \fBvi\fR when neither is set. Change the names, keep one per line and the same order, then save and quit.
.TP
\fB\-\-lower\fR
Make names lower case.
.TP
\fB\-\-upper\fR
Make names upper case.
.TP
\fB\-\-spaces\fR
Turn each run of spaces into one \fB\-\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-number\fR
Add a counter at the first \fB#\fR in the name, or in front as \fB01\-name\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-start N\fR
The first number, 1 by default. Turns on \fB\-\-number\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-width N\fR
Pad numbers to N digits, from 1 to 9. Turns on \fB\-\-number\fR.
.SS Running
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show the old and new names and stop.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Rename without asking. The checks still refuse a clash.
.TP
\fB\-\-undo\fR
Put back the names from the last run.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Show only the result line.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Show each step, and each \fBsed\fR and \fBmv\fR before it runs.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the help.
.SH HOW IT MAKES THE NAMES
.PP
The steps run in a fixed order, so you can combine them:
.IP \(bu 2
The sed expression, or the names you typed in the editor.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-spaces\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-lower\fR or \fB\-\-upper\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-number\fR.
.PP
So \fBbulkrename \-\-lower \-\-spaces 'Tax Return 2025.PDF'\fR gives \fBtax\-return\-2025.pdf\fR.
.PP
With \fB\-\-number\fR, the counter takes the place of the first \fB#\fR in the new name. That is why the sed expression in \fB\-\-number 's/.*\e./scan\-#./'\fR puts a \fB#\fR where the number goes. With no \fB#\fR, the counter goes in front, followed by a \fB\-\fR. Files are numbered in the order you name them, and a shell glob such as \fB*.png\fR sorts them by name.
.PP
The default width is the number of digits in the last number, and at least 2. Ten files get \fB01\fR to \fB10\fR. A hundred get \fB001\fR to \fB100\fR. With \fB\-\-width 1\fR, numbers have no padding.
.PP
A sed expression is anything that looks like \fBs/a/b/\fR or \fBy/abc/xyz/\fR, with any delimiter, such as \fBs|a|b|\fR. You can chain commands with \fB;\fR. The expression must give exactly one line per name. \fBbulkrename\fR refuses with exit 4 when it gives more or fewer, for example after a \fBd\fR or an \fBa\fR command.
.SH SAFETY CHECKS
.PP
\fBbulkrename\fR checks every rename first, and refuses the run with exit 4 when any of these is true:
.IP \(bu 2
Two files would get the same new name.
.IP \(bu 2
A new name belongs to a file that exists and is not part of the rename.
.IP \(bu 2
A new name is empty, \fB.\fR or \fB..\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A new name holds a \fB/\fR. Moving files to another folder is a job for \fBmv\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A name you gave holds a line break.
.PP
It prints each problem, then \fBrefused, nothing renamed.\fR You fix the pattern and run it again.
.PP
Some renames depend on each other. Swapping \fBa.txt\fR and \fBb.txt\fR, or turning \fBf1 f2 f3\fR into \fBf2 f3 f4\fR, would overwrite a file if done in the wrong order. \fBbulkrename\fR sees these swaps and chains. It moves the files that are in the way to a temp name such as \fB.bulkrename\-1087492\-0\fR in the same folder first, then gives every file its new name. A rename that changes only the case, such as \fBREADME.MD\fR to \fBreadme.md\fR, also goes through a temp name on file systems that ignore case, such as a USB stick with FAT or exFAT.
.PP
Every move runs as \fBmv \-n\fR, which never overwrites. After each move, \fBbulkrename\fR checks that the file really moved. When a move fails halfway, it moves the files it already renamed back and exits 1.
.PP
The sed expression runs with \fBsed \-\-sandbox\fR when your sed has it, which GNU sed does. The sandbox refuses the \fBw\fR, \fBr\fR and \fBe\fR commands, so an expression cannot write files or run programs. BusyBox sed, on Alpine, has no sandbox. There \fBbulkrename\fR runs only \fBs\fR and \fBy\fR commands, split by \fB;\fR, with the \fBg\fR, \fBp\fR, \fBi\fR, \fBI\fR, \fBm\fR, \fBM\fR or number flags. Anything else exits 2 before sed runs.
.PP
The question needs a terminal. In a script, pass \fB\-y\fR. Without a terminal and without \fB\-y\fR, \fBbulkrename\fR exits 4.
.SH THE UNDO LIST
.PP
Each run that renames something saves the pairs of old and new full paths to \fB~/.local/state/toolbelt/bulkrename\-undo\fR, or \fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/toolbelt/bulkrename\-undo\fR when that is set. Only the last run is kept.
.PP
\fBbulkrename \-\-undo\fR reads it, shows the renames, asks, and renames back through the same checks. The undo is a rename run of its own, so it saves a new list. A second \fB\-\-undo\fR redoes the first run.
.PP
When a file from the last run has gone, \fB\-\-undo\fR warns and skips it. When a file with the old name has since appeared, the check refuses, the same as for any other clash.
.SH PASS\-THROUGH
.PP
\fBbulkrename\fR takes no options for \fBsed\fR or \fBmv\fR. Put \fB\-\-\fR before file names that start with a dash.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ bulkrename 's/^-//' -- -old.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH NEEDS
.PP
\fBsed\fR and \fBmv\fR. Both come with every distro, from the \fBsed\fR and \fBcoreutils\fR packages, and BusyBox has both. \fB\-e\fR needs a terminal and an editor. Set \fBEDITOR=nano\fR in \fB~/.bashrc\fR if you do not use vi. For VS Code, use \fBEDITOR="code \-\-wait"\fR, so \fBbulkrename\fR waits until you close the file.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Rename camera files
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ bulkrename 's/^IMG_/trip-/' IMG_*.jpg
IMG_4410.jpg -> trip-4410.jpg
IMG_4411.jpg -> trip-4411.jpg
IMG_4412.jpg -> trip-4412.jpg
Rename 3 files? [y/N] y
3 files renamed.
.fi
.RE
.SS Undo it
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ bulkrename --undo
trip-4410.jpg -> IMG_4410.jpg
trip-4411.jpg -> IMG_4411.jpg
trip-4412.jpg -> IMG_4412.jpg
Rename 3 files? [y/N] y
3 files renamed.
.fi
.RE
.SS Clean up names from a scanner or a bank
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ bulkrename -n --lower --spaces *.PDF
Bank  Statement.PDF -> bank-statement.pdf
Tax Return 2025.PDF -> tax-return-2025.pdf
Dry run, nothing renamed.
.fi
.RE
.PP
The two spaces in \fBBank  Statement\fR became one \fB\-\fR.
.SS Number files
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ bulkrename --number 's/.*\e./scan-#./' scan*.png
scan3.png -> scan-01.png
scan4.png -> scan-02.png
Rename 2 files? [y/N] y
2 files renamed.
$ bulkrename -n --start 7 --width 3 IMG_4410.jpg IMG_4411.jpg
IMG_4410.jpg -> 007-IMG_4410.jpg
IMG_4411.jpg -> 008-IMG_4411.jpg
Dry run, nothing renamed.
.fi
.RE
.SS Rename in your editor
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ bulkrename -e IMG_*.jpg
IMG_4411.jpg -> beach.jpg
IMG_4412.jpg -> sunset.jpg
1 name stays the same.
Rename 2 files? [y/N] y
2 files renamed.
.fi
.RE
.PP
The editor showed three lines. Two were changed, and the line for \fBIMG_4410.jpg\fR was left as it was.
.SS Swap two names
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ bulkrename -v 's/^a/X/; s/^b/a/; s/^X/b/' a.txt b.txt
+ sed --sandbox -E -e 's/^a/X/; s/^b/a/; s/^X/b/'
a.txt -> b.txt
b.txt -> a.txt
Rename 2 files? [y/N] y
+ mv -n -- /home/khadir/notes/b.txt /home/khadir/notes/.bulkrename-1087492-1
+ mv -n -- /home/khadir/notes/a.txt /home/khadir/notes/.bulkrename-1087492-0
+ mv -n -- /home/khadir/notes/.bulkrename-1087492-0 /home/khadir/notes/b.txt
+ mv -n -- /home/khadir/notes/.bulkrename-1087492-1 /home/khadir/notes/a.txt
2 files renamed.
.fi
.RE
.SS A clash is refused
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ bulkrename 's/-v[0-9]+//' report-v1.pdf report-v2.pdf
bulkrename: report-v1.pdf and report-v2.pdf would both become report.pdf
bulkrename: refused, nothing renamed.
$ echo $?
4
.fi
.RE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
\fBbulkrename: IMG_4410.jpg would become trip\-4410.jpg, which already exists\fR : Another file already has that name. Move it, or change the pattern so the names differ.
.PP
`\fBbulkrename: sed could not read the expression: \-e expression #1, char 6: unterminated \fRs' command`\fB : The expression has a mistake. Quote it with single quotes, so the shell leaves \fR*\fB, \fR$\fB and \fR\e` alone.
.PP
\fBbulkrename: sed could not read the expression: ... e/r/w commands disabled in sandbox mode\fR : The expression tried to write a file or run a command. Use only \fBs\fR and \fBy\fR.
.PP
\fBbulkrename: the list had 3 names and now has 2. Keep one name per line, nothing renamed\fR : A line was deleted in the editor. Keep a line for each file, and leave the ones you do not want to change as they are.
.PP
\fBbulkrename: nothing to undo, no run is recorded in /home/khadir/.local/state/toolbelt/bulkrename\-undo\fR : No run has renamed anything yet, or the state folder was cleaned.
.PP
Nothing matched : \fBsed \-E\fR uses extended regular expressions. Write \fBs/(IMG)_([0\-9]+)/\e2\-\e1/\fR, not \fBs/\e(IMG\e)_/.../\fR. Test the pattern with \fB\-n\fR first.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
It worked, or there was nothing to rename.
.TP
1
It failed. A file is missing, a move failed, or there is nothing to undo.
.TP
2
Bad usage, such as a broken sed expression or \fB\-\-lower\fR with \fB\-\-upper\fR.
.TP
3
\fBsed\fR or \fBmv\fR is missing.
.TP
4
Refused. The names clash, the editor list changed length, or there is no terminal to ask in.
.TP
5
You answered no.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBmv(1)\fR, \fBsed(1)\fR, \fBrename(1)\fR
